00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.270 align:start position:0%
 
new york times bestselling author  ta-nehisi coates is sharing his personal  journey with  younger readers his new book the  beautiful struggle was adapted from his  2008 memoir of the same name to appeal  to young adults it's about growing up in  west baltimore  in the 1980s coates writes about how he  succeeded and became the man he is with  the help of his parents  tough love hussey coats joins us now  good morning thank you so much for being  here  thanks for having me what kind of a  challenge is it to take something that  you wrote is the first book you wrote i  think 12 what are we 12  13 years ago now and and 12 years going  on with 13.  can you believe it  tom flies time flies man yeah sure thing  but  so so how did you what was your approach  in adapting this for for younger readers  well i i actually i had quite a bit of  help adapting it but i i think um  one of the the the really fortunate  things about it  when i wrote it at the time i think i  was 31 when beautiful struggle published  and and  what i what i always imagined was that  it was a younger version of me say in my  early 20s  writing to a high school slash middle  school version of me  yeah so it was always supposed to be the  book that i would have wanted to see  at that period of time so in many ways i  think that the foundation was there  yeah that's that's such an interesting  state of mind to put you i think so too  but tahasi when i look at your life  you've had really quite a life and i  wonder if you were hard on yourself  because you describe yourself going to  school as a monument to unknowledge  fat cheeks that you talked a lot in  second grade they actually diagnosed you  as  probably mentally challenged would be a  very polite way of saying  wow but your mom always believed she  your mom always believed she never gave  up on you but  and i look at you do you ever marvel  about how your life turned out when you  consider the background  of how you started i i'm saying kudos  and bravo to your mom  i do i do you know i i had a you know a  great mother i mean my mom taught me to  read she's probably the most  consequential figure in terms of me me  being a writer in the first place  um but you know you know what i i have  to say gail i think  like if i had to completely write that  book  again now i would have been much more  understanding of the younger me um  it's telling that it was published that  you know uh you know originally almost  12 years ago because you know i've now  had some experience some time my own son  is now 20 years old  you know what i mean i i know you know  what what kids are actually like  and i think a lot in that book is me  trying to figure out what's wrong with  me  and if i could say anything to my  younger self now i would say nothing  nothing at all nothing at all  you write about you write about how your  mom was always looking towards mecca  which is  howard city which i i love that idea  and and she said what i could what you  write is i what i could not understand  was that she believed that i was owed  that no matter what i done in high  school  somehow i was entitled to see the mecca  to find my place in this great  black cosmo cosmopolis where does  as you look back now what was the root  of that faith  in that she had do you think so i think  first of all my mom you know herself she  you know i  was a graduate of hbcu cop in you know  in  in in baltimore and i think um having  that reference point a lot of you know  her you know sisters and relatives who'd  gone  had gone to you know hbcus university of  maryland uh eastern shore and so that  was  you know i think that sense in general  of hbcus  as a kind of you know place of of haven  you know for for black kids but i think  more than that and this goes back to  you know what i was saying before i  think at that time my mom  really had that kind of forgiveness and  tenderness that i wish i had had when i  when i when i wrote the book  um she probably had you know some great  understanding herself having been a  teacher  that you know wait where you start is  not who you  where you end up and 16 year old you is  not a verdict  on who you ultimately will be we're  looking we just saw a shot of you when  you were at howard but i really i got  such a kick out of your mom because  she you wrote that she said you wanted  to you didn't want to go to howard at  first  and your mom gave you that i didn't your  mom gave you that you know what this is  smile when she was talking about what's  going to happen here  that when you met when you made a  mistake she made you do an essay to  write over and over what you did and how  you did it and why you did it  but i want to talk to you tom ozzy about  the current events because  all of us are still reeling and so  rocked by what happened at the capitol  on january 6 well i'll remember we will  never  ever forget what went through your mind  when you saw that unfold at the united  states capital  uh  this is horrible to say we told you so  i wouldn't even say i told you so we  told you so who's it i think there have  been a number of  the we as i would say african-american  writers and you know african-americans  at large i think there were a number of  people  um that in the wake of donald trump's  election  uh were very very clear about the great  danger  uh that you know a a trump presidency  would pose  uh to not just to black people but to  the united states itself and  we you know we're told that you know  we're making too much out of this  you know oh he's not really a white  supremacist oh it's not that bad oh the  office of the presidency  will sober him oh there people around  him who will make sure  you know and curb his power and  his presidency ends with him inciting an  attack  on the congress of the united states of  america  hopefully people will take this  seriously now what do you think  how how significant do you think the  damage is from this  oh i think it's quite significant i  think it's quite significant because  if i i i don't have the exact numbers  but i believe ultimately even after that  attack  um at least half perhaps a majority  of the republican congress people in the  house of representatives  against you know uh the idea of  affirming the vote  people still supported him yes still  supported and so and so you you have to  say that there is  you know um the riot can almost be a  distraction the insurrection can be a  distraction  there is a deeply felt sense in a  certain  population in this country that we  should have a minoritarian form of  government  you know um and that that's not going  away okay i apologize we have to go  i so appreciate you being here the  beautiful struggle is the book thomas  coates thank you so much  for joining us we'll be right back good  to see you

